The so-called megatooth shark, Otodus megalodon, is an iconic extinct shark in the fossil record and may have reached up to 24.3 m in total length (TL). The TL estimation of 24.3 m was partly based on published photographs of an associated set of 10.8-million-year-old vertebrae from Gram, Denmark, some of which allegedly measured 23 cm in diameter. The specimen was thought lost after its excavation, but we report here on its rediscovery, confirming the reported maximum diameter of 23 cm. The vertebrae were found with remains of a fossil basking shark, but our analyses suggest that the vertebrae belong to O. megalodon, with the possibility that the fossil basking shark remains represent the stomach content of the megatooth shark. The vertebrae were used to decipher the developmental growth pattern of this individual, and our analysis suggests that O. megalodon theoretically could have grown even slightly larger than 24.3 m. However, the growth model presented in this study must be viewed as highly tentative. More importantly, we strongly suggest retaining “24.3 m TL” as presently the best scientifically justifiable estimated maximum size for O. megalodon. Nevertheless, the O. megalodon specimen described in this study is significant because it represents the largest shark vertebral specimen known to date, and to our knowledge, the largest fish vertebrae ever recorded. Ultimately, a more complete skeletal specimen of O. megalodon, ideally with associated teeth, is needed for any further discussion regarding the maximum possible size of the extinct megatooth shark.
